1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,998
Commentable expressions are very easy and simple

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,496
CTs essentially help us write readable and maintainable code quickly

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,000
By definition they are a temporary named result set

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,395
In reality CTs are great because the result remains in memory

5
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:26,194
during the execution of a SELECT INSERT UPDATE DELETE or MERGE statement

6
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,594
The syntax of a CT would look something like this

7
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:39,591
The with clause is basically telling the database that we are going to create a commentable expression

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:45,897
after which you can give a name to our CT or more precisely to our result set

9
00:00:45,897 --> 00:00:48,188
that we can reference later

10
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:54,650
The column names are completely optional and they are used for setting up aliases

11
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:58,700
for the column names that will come out of the commentable expression

12
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:06,455
The CTE query is a SELECT statement and in reality this is just a temporary table

13
00:01:06,455 --> 00:01:11,697
that will be referenced from the FROM clause like other everyday tables

14
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:19,300
The reference to CTE part is where we will execute and reference our commentable expression

15
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,597
Let's take a look at an example

16
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:29,495
Let's say we extracted raw data from the source systems via Airbnb

17
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:35,296
The data is 100 uncleaned and we will want to do transformations on it

18
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:41,500
In step 1 we create the commentable expression that we named raw listings

19
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:47,986
Then in step 2 we have an inner query where we selected the source listings as they are

20
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,596
All the columns that we could get our hands on basically

21
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:58,178
Then in step 3 which is an outer query we query the CT name that we have provided

22
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:05,889
which is raw listings We selected not all columns of the source but only specific columns

23
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:09,895
on which we performed the simplest of all changes

24
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:15,799
a rename of the ID column to listing id to make it more obvious That's it

25
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,022
Now keep in mind that we won't dive into more advanced CTs

26
00:02:20,022 --> 00:02:22,698
as they are outside the scope of this course

27
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:27,200
However some general takeaways I would like to share

28
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,737
So although a CT has very similar functionality to a view

29
00:02:32,737 --> 00:02:36,890
CTs will not store the definition in metadata

30
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:41,000
We prefer CTs because they are extremely readable

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,500
they are easy to maintain and they make complex queries more understandable

32
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:55,299
Furthermore a given query can be divided into separate simple logical building blocks

33
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:02,100
which can be then used to build more complex CTs until a final result set gets generated

34
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:10,090
Lastly CTs can be also defined in functions stored procedures triggers or even views

35
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,872
We're going to use CTs many times throughout the course

36
00:03:14,872 --> 00:03:17,292
so you will see a ton of examples soon

37
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,296
See you in the next video
